  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual overview of coastal and ocean “eco-engineering” approaches for carbon removal and related biogeochemical benefits. It shows a land-to-sea system in which nutrient management on land and wastewater treatment near shore help reduce nutrient inputs, while multiple marine interventions operate offshore.\n\nAt the center, a seaweed cultivation area appears to be a major feature, linked to carbon capture in biomass and export of organic material downward. Nearby labels suggest different carbon pathways, including particulate organic carbon sinking, refractory dissolved organic carbon, and authigenic carbonate formation in deeper water. The image also includes engineered structures in the water and arrows indicating transport or sequestration processes.\n\nSeveral enhancement methods are shown around the farmed coastal zone: clay and alkalinity addition from a vessel, artificial upwelling from below, and iron–aluminum fertilization farther offshore to stimulate marine productivity. A dark plume spreading across the surface seems to indicate altered water properties or a treatment zone extending from the coast.\n\nOverall, the figure compares a portfolio of linked land-based and marine interventions aimed at boosting biological production, improving water chemistry, and increasing long-term carbon sequestration in coastal and open-ocean environments.",
